-- #1110 PR C corpus — DECLARE AND RE-EXPORT THE SAME NAME.  This module pins a
-- PRE-EXISTING mis-attribution, filed as #1245.
--
-- `mid` re-exports `deep`'s `Relay` AND declares a `Relay` of its own.  What a
-- consumer writing `import mid.{Relay, hop}` actually binds is **mid's own**
-- interface — verified by method identity on the binary this fixture was written
-- against, not inferred:
--
--     import mid.{Relay, hop} ; impl Relay Int where hop n = n   -> exit 0
--     import mid.{Relay, via} ; impl Relay Int where via n = n
--       -> "Method 'via' is not part of interface 'Relay'", exit 1
--
-- But `known["mid"]` is built by `ifaceOriginExports` as
-- `[("iface:Relay","mid"), … re-exports …]` and the re-export half contributes
-- `("iface:Relay","deep")`; `omFromPairs` is last-write-wins, so `bindTypeOrigin`
-- hands a consumer `iface:Relay -> OriginModule "deep"`.
--
-- 🚨 SO THE GOLDEN'S `iface:Relay 1 mod:deep` IS THE WRONG ANSWER, PINNED ON
-- PURPOSE.  It records current behaviour so the defect is VISIBLE rather than
-- merely described in a comment, exactly as the CONFLICT rows pin #1223.  It is
-- expected to move to `mod:mid` when #1245 lands; whoever fixes that re-blesses
-- this fixture and says so.
--
-- ⚠️ THE DEFECT IS PRE-EXISTING AND NOT SPECIFIC TO INTERFACES.  The type
-- namespace has always had it, for the same reason and in the same fold — also
-- verified first-hand: two modules declaring `data Bar`, the second re-exporting
-- the first's, a consumer binding the SECOND's constructor while the occurrence is
-- attributed to the first.  #1245 covers both; fixing only the interface half here
-- would have moved goldens beyond this layer and a red would not have named its
-- culprit.

export import deep.{Relay}

export interface Relay a where
  hop : a -> Int
